<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=sericulture">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Tools and materials</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sericulture</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sericulture is the raising of silkworms for the production of raw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, typically by feeding them on mulberry leaves. It originated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and was long a closely guarded skill. Sericulture supplemented agriculture as part of the Ming economy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sericulture.jpg" alt="Sericulture" width="320" height="191" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to Liang Kai · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Soaking_the_cocoons_and_reeling_the_silk_(Sericulture_by_Liang_Kai,_1200s).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Sericulture, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> farming, is the cultivation of silkworms to produce silk. The caterpillar of the domestic silkmoth is the species most widely used and intensively studied. It is no longer found in the wild, because selective breeding has left most of these moths flightless and without defence against predators. Silk is believed to have first been produced in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> as early as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> period.</p><p>According to a Confucian text the discovery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> production dates to about 2700 BCE, while archaeological records point to silk cultivation as early as the Yangshao period, 5000 to 3000 BCE. A ceramic piece made to look like a silkworm, found in 1977 at Nancun in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a> and 5,400 to 5,500 years old, is the earliest known evidence. Silk fibre found on Indus Civilization sites dating to 2450 to 2000 BCE suggests that silk was used over a wide region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>By about the first half of the 1st century CE the practice had reached ancient Khotan through a series of interactions along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>, and by 140 CE it was established in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. In the 6th century CE the smuggling of silkworm eggs into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> led to its establishment in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, where it remained a monopoly of the Byzantine Empire for centuries.</p><p>In 1147, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-crusade">Second Crusade</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-ii-of-sicily">Roger II of Sicily</a> attacked Corinth and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>, two important centres of Byzantine silk production. He captured the weavers and their equipment and set up his own silkworks in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a>, which eventually spread the industry to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The silkworms are fed with mulberry leaves, and after the fourth moult they climb a twig and spin their cocoons. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> is a continuous filament of fibroin protein, secreted from two salivary glands in the head of each worm, held together by a gum called sericin. Placing the cocoons in hot water removes the sericin, a step known as degumming, and frees the filaments for reeling. The hot water also kills the pupa inside.</p><p>One cocoon holds approximately 1,000 yards of filament, and about 2,500 silkworms are needed to produce a pound of raw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a> criticised silk production on the philosophy of ahimsa, not to hurt any living thing, and promoted silk made without boiling the pupa and wild silk from the cocoons of wild and semiwild moths.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sericulture” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tipu established state trading enterprises, sought commercial privileges through embassies to the Ottoman sultan and to France, regulated weights, measures and coinage, and promoted <b>sericulture</b>, which became a lasting industry of the region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mysore-1399-1799">Kingdom of Mysore (1399-1799) · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The economy rested on agriculture: rice, wheat and millet, supplemented by cotton, <b>sericulture</b> and, from the late 16th century, New World crops such as maize and sweet potatoes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The people may also have practised an early form of <b>sericulture</b>, the raising of silkworms.</blockquote><a href="/culture/yangshao-culture">Yangshao culture · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1767 Uesugi Harunori, known as Yozan, imposed frugality, encouraged <b>sericulture</b>, weaving, lacquer trees and carp ponds, reclaimed land and revived the Kojokan school, restoring the finances over decades.</blockquote><a href="/state/yonezawa-domain">Yonezawa Domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 3500 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Nancun, Hebei, China</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>A Confucian text dates silk to about 2700 BCE; archaeology points to the Yangshao period, 5000–3000 BCE.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic-revolution">Agriculture<span>c. 10,000 BCE</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/kite">Kite<span>by 549</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/jacquard-machine">Jacquard machine<span>1804</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=sericulture">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
